  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Two massive warships vanished in the Arctic with 129 men. In 1845, HMS Erebus and Terror set sail to map the Northwest Passage. Then, total silence.",
        "visual_keywords": "stormy icy ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Search parties discovered frozen graves on Beechey Island and a handwritten note on King William Island explaining the ships were trapped in ice.",
        "visual_keywords": "snowy arctic tundra"
      },
      {
        "narration": "For nearly 170 years, searchers scanned millions of square miles without finding either ship. Were they looking in the completely wrong direction?",
        "visual_keywords": "barren frozen landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2014, underwater archaeologists finally discovered HMS Erebus intact on the ocean floor, followed by HMS Terror in 2016. Their locations matched accounts passed down by Inuit historians.",
        "visual_keywords": "sonar screen underwater"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Inuit hunters had described the wreck in Terror Bay for generations before sonar proved them right. Why did it take searchers 170 years to finally listen?",
        "visual_keywords": "foggy arctic horizon"
      }
    ],
